The 12 outgoing wires totaled $876,400.

The remaining incoming funds were used for payroll, established suppliers, tax payments, operating expenses, transfers between Alder Peak accounts, cryptocurrency purchases described below, and balances remaining in the account.

The bank identified no significant cash deposits or withdrawals associated with the reviewed activity.

Granite Coast reviewed the newly observed counterparties.

Pacific Meridian Trading Ltd. appeared in Hong Kong commercial records as an active trading company. Publicly available information described the company as an importer and exporter of electronic components, industrial equipment, and general merchandise.

Eastern Bridge Components Ltd. appeared in Singapore corporate records and maintained a website advertising industrial electronics, control systems, and automation components.

Nova Equipment Partners LLC was an active California limited liability company formed in November 2025. Its public website described machinery sourcing and equipment brokerage services.

Cascade Procurement Services LLC was an active Oregon company formed in January 2026. Its public information described procurement and logistics support for commercial customers.

Granite Coast identified no sanctions matches involving Alder Peak, Brooks, Owens, or the four primary outgoing counterparties.

On May 6, 2026, Granite Coast contacted Brooks regarding the increased incoming activity and new counterparties.

Brooks stated that Alder Peak had recently expanded into procurement services for customers seeking specialized machinery and electronic control systems. He said some customers were unable to source equipment directly and paid Alder Peak to locate and acquire products through distributors.

Brooks stated that Alder Peak earned margins of approximately 8 to 15 percent on these transactions.

Brooks provided four customer invoices and three supplier invoices.

The customer invoices identified Sterling Commercial Interiors LLC, Harbor West Engineering Inc., Summit Office Technologies LLC, and Meridian Dental Systems Inc. The invoices described equipment procurement, industrial control components, and specialized machinery.

The supplier invoices identified Pacific Meridian Trading Ltd., Nova Equipment Partners LLC, and Eastern Bridge Components Ltd. Invoice amounts generally corresponded to related outgoing wires.

Granite Coast was able to verify that the named companies existed but did not independently confirm delivery of all goods described in the invoices.

On May 23, 2026, Granite Coast received a fraud-related inquiry from the financial institution that originated the May 19 $65,300 wire from Clearwater Mechanical Contractors Inc.

The originating institution advised that its customer reported receiving altered vendor-payment instructions through a compromised email account. According to the inquiry, Clearwater Mechanical Contractors intended to pay an established supplier but received fraudulent instructions directing payment to Alder Peak Equipment LLC instead.

Granite Coast reviewed the $65,300 payment and determined that approximately $51,000 of the funds remained in Alder Peak’s account when the inquiry was received. Granite Coast restricted that amount pending review and subsequently returned $49,800 to the originating financial institution after accounting for intervening account activity.

Granite Coast has not independently verified the full circumstances reported by Clearwater Mechanical Contractors.

On June 3, 2026, Granite Coast contacted Brooks regarding the fraud inquiry.

Brooks stated that he was surprised by the allegation and believed the payment represented a legitimate equipment order arranged through a referral partner.

Brooks stated that an individual identifying himself as “David Chen” had contacted Alder Peak by email and represented that he worked with commercial customers needing industrial equipment sourcing.

Brooks stated that Chen introduced several of the new customers and provided information concerning equipment requirements.

Brooks provided email communications with an individual using the address david.chen@industrialsourcepartners.com.

The emails contained requests for equipment quotations, customer names, payment amounts, and supplier instructions. Brooks stated that he had never met Chen in person.

Granite Coast’s open-source review did not identify a registered U.S. company named Industrial Source Partners associated with the email domain. The website associated with the domain contained limited information and no verifiable physical office address.

Brooks stated that he had not conducted independent due diligence on Chen beyond reviewing the website and communicating by email.

On June 18, 2026, Granite Coast received a second fraud inquiry, this time involving the June 14 $62,500 wire from Lakeshore Facility Management Inc.

The originating financial institution advised that Lakeshore reported that an employee email account had been compromised and that fraudulent payment instructions caused the company to send funds to Alder Peak rather than an intended vendor.

Granite Coast identified approximately $18,700 remaining in the account that appeared traceable to the incoming Lakeshore payment and restricted the funds during review.

Granite Coast has not received fraud notifications concerning the other 16 incoming wires.

Following the second fraud inquiry, Granite Coast expanded its review of Alder Peak’s account activity.

The bank identified that Brooks had opened an institutional cryptocurrency account in the name of Alder Peak Equipment LLC at Northline Digital Exchange on June 20, 2026.

Between June 22 and August 18, 2026, Alder Peak transferred $247,000 from Granite Coast account 58392017 to Northline Digital Exchange through five ACH transfers:

June 22: $42,000.
July 6: $51,000.
July 22: $46,000.
August 5: $58,000.
August 18: $50,000.

The five transfers totaled $247,000.

Granite Coast contacted Northline Digital Exchange through established financial-crime information-sharing channels and received information indicating that the funds were used primarily to purchase USDC.

Northline advised that cryptocurrency purchased through Alder Peak’s exchange account was subsequently withdrawn to external blockchain addresses.

Granite Coast obtained blockchain transaction identifiers associated with the withdrawals and reviewed the transactions using its blockchain analytics provider.

The June 22 transfer to Northline resulted in the purchase of approximately $40,900 in USDC, of which approximately $40,500 was withdrawn on June 23 to external wallet 0x7F42D1B963A805CE417E29F6508C2D31A684B790.

The July 6 transfer resulted in the purchase of approximately $49,800 in USDC, of which approximately $49,300 was withdrawn on July 7 to the same external wallet.

The July 22 transfer resulted in the purchase of approximately $44,900 in USDC, of which approximately $44,500 was withdrawn on July 23 to wallet 0x2A91E7C4B650D831F72C5089A143E6D479BC2051.

The August 5 transfer resulted in the purchase of approximately $56,700 in USDC, of which approximately $56,200 was withdrawn on August 6 to wallet 0x7F42D1B963A805CE417E29F6508C2D31A684B790.

The August 18 transfer resulted in the purchase of approximately $48,800 in USDC, of which approximately $48,300 was withdrawn on August 19 to wallet 0x2A91E7C4B650D831F72C5089A143E6D479BC2051.

The difference between fiat transferred to Northline and cryptocurrency withdrawn was attributable to balances remaining at the exchange, transaction costs, and trading activity.

Northline advised that the withdrawals were initiated from Alder Peak’s authenticated exchange account. Login activity showed devices previously associated with the account. Northline did not identify evidence of account takeover.

Granite Coast’s blockchain review showed that wallet 0x7F42D1B963A805CE417E29F6508C2D31A684B790 received the three withdrawals initiated on June 23, July 7, and August 6.

Within approximately one to six hours of receipt, portions of the cryptocurrency were transferred from that wallet to multiple additional addresses.

Two of those downstream addresses subsequently sent funds to deposit addresses associated by the blockchain analytics provider with a major offshore cryptocurrency exchange.

Granite Coast identified no sanctions attribution or known illicit-service attribution associated with wallet 0x7F42D1B963A805CE417E29F6508C2D31A684B790.

The blockchain analytics provider did not identify the owner or controller of that wallet.

Granite Coast also reviewed wallet 0x2A91E7C4B650D831F72C5089A143E6D479BC2051, which received the July 23 and August 19 withdrawals.

Funds received by that wallet were subsequently divided among several additional addresses. One downstream address later interacted with a deposit address associated by the analytics provider with Pacific Meridian Trading Ltd.’s known cryptocurrency activity.

Based on that downstream interaction, Granite Coast determined that wallet 0x2A91E7C4B650D831F72C5089A143E6D479BC2051 was owned and controlled by Pacific Meridian Trading Ltd. and that Alder Peak was using cryptocurrency to make additional payments directly to Pacific Meridian outside the traditional banking system.

Granite Coast’s blockchain analytics provider did not independently attribute wallet 0x2A91E7C4B650D831F72C5089A143E6D479BC2051 to Pacific Meridian Trading Ltd.

On August 7, 2026, Granite Coast contacted Brooks regarding the cryptocurrency activity.

Brooks stated that David Chen had suggested cryptocurrency as an alternative way to pay certain overseas suppliers because settlement was faster than international wires.

Brooks stated that he transferred funds to Northline, purchased USDC, and sent the assets to wallet addresses provided by Chen.

Brooks said he believed the addresses were associated with suppliers but acknowledged that he did not independently verify ownership of the wallets.

Brooks stated that he did not personally control the external destination wallets.

Brooks provided several emails from Chen containing wallet addresses and requested payment amounts. The messages did not identify the legal owner of each wallet.

Granite Coast compared the wallet addresses contained in the messages with blockchain records and confirmed that the addresses matched the destinations used by Alder Peak.

The bank also reviewed the timing of the cryptocurrency activity relative to incoming customer payments.

The June 22 transfer to Northline occurred three business days after the May 19 Clearwater payment had been substantially depleted through other account activity and eight days after the June 14 Lakeshore payment.

The July and August cryptocurrency transfers followed additional incoming wires from customers introduced or referenced in Brooks’s communications with Chen.

Granite Coast could not determine whether specific units of cryptocurrency purchased at Northline were funded by proceeds from individual incoming wires because the funds were commingled in Alder Peak’s operating account.

On August 12, 2026, Granite Coast requested additional commercial documentation from Brooks.

Brooks provided five additional customer invoices, four supplier invoices, shipping records associated with three equipment deliveries, and email correspondence concerning equipment specifications.

Two shipping records showed delivery of industrial components to Alder Peak’s Spokane warehouse. A third showed equipment shipped directly from an overseas supplier to one of Alder Peak’s established U.S. customers.

The documentation provided evidence that at least some of Alder Peak’s expanded procurement activity involved actual goods.

However, Granite Coast could not match every new incoming customer payment to a specific shipment, purchase order, or completed transaction.

One of the businesses associated with a fraud complaint, Lakeshore Facility Management Inc., did not appear in the shipping records provided by Brooks.

Granite Coast also identified that several customers introduced through David Chen had no prior commercial relationship with Alder Peak before April 2026.

Granite Coast’s review of Alder Peak’s historical business activity showed that the company continued receiving payments from longstanding customers during the same period and continued making payroll, tax, freight, insurance, and established-supplier payments.

The bank therefore did not conclude that Alder Peak lacked legitimate business operations.

Granite Coast considers the activity suspicious because Alder Peak experienced a substantial and rapid increase in payments from previously unrelated businesses, including two payments reported by originating institutions as potentially resulting from compromised business email accounts.

Alder Peak subsequently moved significant funds through newly observed domestic and international counterparties and began purchasing cryptocurrency for transfer to external wallet addresses provided by an online intermediary whom Brooks had not independently verified.

The activity is consistent with possible use of an otherwise legitimate business as an intermediary for movement of fraud proceeds, possible business email compromise proceeds, and potentially unlicensed third-party payment activity.

The cryptocurrency activity further increases Granite Coast’s concern because Brooks acknowledged sending funds to wallet addresses provided by an intermediary without independently establishing who controlled those addresses.

Granite Coast has not established that all incoming payments were fraudulent, that Brooks knowingly participated in an underlying fraud scheme, that the newly observed commercial counterparties were knowingly involved in fraudulent activity, or that the external cryptocurrency wallets were controlled by the parties Brooks believed he was paying.

Granite Coast restricted cryptocurrency-related transfers and certain outgoing international wires on August 24, 2026 and placed Alder Peak’s account under enhanced review. The account remains open.

Granite Coast maintains account statements, ACH and wire records, customer and supplier invoices, shipping documentation, customer communications, fraud inquiries, public-record research, cryptocurrency exchange information, blockchain transaction identifiers, blockchain analytics results, customer due diligence records, and related investigative materials and will make those records available to appropriate authorities upon request.
